          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "The Optimizer Bonus",
          "text": "An exclusive `&mut` lets LLVM assume `noalias` — an optimization C compilers cannot make unless you hand-write `restrict`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Where It Genuinely Hurts",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Doubly-linked lists, graphs and observers are not reference trees.",
            "They do not typecheck without indices, `Rc` or `unsafe`.",
            "Aliasing violations via raw pointers are UB even inside `unsafe`.",
            "Validate those with `cargo +nightly miri test`."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Anti-Patterns To Drop",
          "items": [
            "Cloning reflexively before reading the error.",
            "`Rc<RefCell<_>>` on every field of every struct.",
            "Adding lifetimes until the message disappears.",
            "Sprinkling `'static` bounds to silence E0521."
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "The Model To Keep",
          "text": "A borrow is a promise about a region of code, and the checker's only job is verifying that promise. Read the error's three locations, name which of the two rules broke, then walk the ladder: shorten, split, copy, restructure, clone."
        }
